WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.560
Researchers now estimate Mars' North Pole ice contains only 3% dust by menace instead of the prior 25% figure. This is fascinating frontiers, episode 210. Planetary scientists have revised their picture of the ice at Mars' North Pole. They combined measurements from the Omega spectrometer on Mars' express, the chrism instrument on Mars' reconnaissance orbiter, and the surface stereo imager that flew on the Phoenix lander.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:32.500
The new calculation shows the top layer holds only 3% dust by mass.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.380
Earlier models had assumed 25% dust by mass.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.320
The difference comes from recognizing that the ice sits in distinct layers with thin

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.680
dust sheets between them.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:48.220
Seasonal frost that forms in winter carries more dust while summer exposes cleaner, older

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:48.840
ice beneath.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.680
The study appears in NPJ space exploration.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.460
Next up, a study of astronaut health after long flights.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.960
Researchers reviewed medical records for U.S. astronauts and found higher hip fracture rates

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:07.740
after missions longer than 90 days. They used Bayesian probabilistic modeling to work around

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:13.560
the small number of people who have flown in space. Lead investigator Gene D. Sabanga at NASA

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:19.200
Johnson Space Center noted that astronauts often feel recovered and returned to demanding activities

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.960
even though bone loss continues. Co-author Matthew T. Drake pointed out that prior studies were

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.760
limited by small sample sizes. The work is published in Mayo Clinic proceedings and calls for more

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:37.260
attention to long-term skeletal health. SpaceX has a packed launch day planned. A Falcon 9 will

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:43.920
lift crew 13 from Cape Canaveral at 1110 AM Eastern. Later, a second Falcon 9 will fly the

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:51.020
transporter 18 ride share from Vandenberg, carrying 130 payloads. A Falcon Heavy will then launch the

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:58.340
classified NROL 97 mission from Kennedy Space Center at 11.53pm Eastern. The company has

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:04.340
completed a triple launch in 13 hours only once before. The schedule follows the first orbital

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:10.420
flight of Starship Test Article 14. Astronomers have captured the first direct image of a

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:16.580
protoplanet still embedded in its birth disk. The Atacama large millimeter sub-millimeter array

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:21.040
revealed Wispit-2B at 57 astronomical units from its star.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.660
The object is roughly five times the mass of Jupiter

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.240
and lies 430 light-years away.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.380
A second proto-planet, Wispit-2C,

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.520
orbits closer at 15 astronomical units

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.420
with a mass between 8 and 12 Jupiter masses.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:40.060
Lead author Miriam Benisti reported clear signs

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.520
of both planets carving gaps and cavities

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.380
while gas swirls appear around the outer body.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:50.800
Those swirls match structures long predicted by computer models but never seen before.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:54.300
The image is published in the astrophysical journal letters.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:59.640
A different team has found evidence that even a relatively cool lava world can hold an

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:00.080
atmosphere.

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:07.020
They observe the secondary eclipse of HD3167B with the James Webb Space Telescope.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:16.080
The planet is Earth-sized, orbits its K-type star every 0.96 Earth days, and lies 154 light years away.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.300
Its measured day-side temperature came in cooler than expected, pointing to heat being

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:28.040
carried to the night side. The result challenges the assumption that only the hottest lava worlds can sustain atmospheres.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.500
The paper appears in the astrophysical journal letters.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:37.180
Bulgaria and Prime Minister Rumin Radev spoke at the Euro news defense and space summit

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:37.680
in Brussels.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.280
He recalled that Bulgaria was the sixth country to send a citizen into orbit.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.800
He highlighted the work of the Bulgarian company in Durosat as a current example of national

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:48.480
capability.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:54.440
Radev noted that two decades ago, space access required enormous financial and industrial

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:55.180
resources.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:00.120
Advances in technology and artificial intelligence have now broadened opportunities

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:06.220
for more nations. Galileo space is developing satellites that process signals while still in orbit.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:11.680
The design aims to deliver answers about events and locations without waiting for data to reach

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:18.100
the ground. Defense and commercial operators are the intended users. A Japanese lead team flew the

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:24.600
Sunrise III instrument on a stratospheric balloon in 2024. The observatory recorded intricate

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:29.120
magnetic structures above regions of the Sun previously considered magnetically quiet.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:33.260
The observations are detailed in the astrophysical journal letters.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.920
A new study connects some minute-long x-ray flashes to mergers of neutron stars.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:43.680
Researchers had long associated only short gamma ray bursts with these collisions.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:49.020
The work published in science bulletin suggests certain longer flashes may originate from

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:49.840
the same events.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:55.800
A recent NASA policy update has raised concerns about increased risk of contaminating Mars.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:00.620
Critics argue the change relaxes earlier planetary protection standards.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:08.060
Observers have expressed doubt that Russia will meet its announced timeline for a launch abort test of its next-generation crew spacecraft.

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:10.940
The test is currently scheduled for next year.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:16.640
An Iowa Casino opening in November will include an interactive space exploration exhibit.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:23.140
The Compass Center display covers astronaut training, rocket launches, microgravity, and life in space.

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:27.960
It was created by Cytek in Perth and produced by Imagine Exhibitions.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:33.500
NASA has selected three new science investigations for its planned lunar surface outpost.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.900
The selections expand research planned for sustained operations on the Moon.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:43.660
Canadian Space Agency astronaut Joshua Kutrick reports he is in high spirits ahead of

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:44.740
the crew 13 launch.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.500
He will join three crewmates for a six-month stay on the International Space Station.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:55.720
The Dragon spacecraft for Crew 13 is targeted to reach the station in 7 hours and 50 minutes.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:00.840
A successful transit would set a new record for the fastest US trip to the station.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:07.300
Docking is planned for about 7 pm eastern on October 1st. The proto-planet image moves the

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:12.600
field beyond computer models into direct observation of gas flows around forming worlds.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:20.040
Wispit 2B and its companion are actively shaping their disc at distances of 15 and 57 astronomical

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:20.820
units.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:26.220
The Atacama large millimeter sub-millimeter array captured structures that simulations

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:27.580
had predicted for years.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:32.920
This single view supplies a benchmark for how planets interact with their birth environment.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:38.160
It also raises the question of how many other young systems will show similar features at

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:39.260
comparable resolution.

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:45.540
What fraction of protoplanetary discs will reveal these interactions once observed at matching detail?

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:51.320
A single strand of solar magnetic field can stretch across distances that would span the continental

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:57.620
United States if laid end-to-end on Earth. The Sunrise 3 balloon flight lifted its instrument

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:02.740
suite above most of the atmosphere and recorded magnetic structures above areas once thought

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:08.220
magnetically simple. Those structures twist and reconnect on scales smaller than a typical

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.860
sunspot, yet still channel energy upward into the corona.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:17.020
The data showed field lines that fold back on themselves in patterns no current model

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:18.160
fully reproduces.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:24.440
One open question is why the quiet sun maintains such complexity when the underlying convection

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:25.440
should smooth it out.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:31.060
Keep an eye on the three SpaceX launches scheduled for today and any updates on the crew

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:32.480
13 docking timeline.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:35.320
That covers today's space and science news.

